<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Iskolat</h1><p class="st-meta">1917 CE – 1918 CE · capital Valka</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Soviet government of unoccupied Latvia in 1917-1918, backed by the Latvian Riflemen, the first Latvian soviet state.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the Bolshevik seizure of power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> in November 1917, Latvian Bolsheviks, strongly supported by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvian-riflemen">Latvian Riflemen</a>, formed the Executive Committee of the Soviet of Workers, Soldiers and Landless of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Latvia</a>, known by its Russian acronym Iskolat. It governed the part of Latvia not occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, with its seat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valka">Valka</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesis">Cēsis</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Iskolat declared the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-germans">Baltic German</a> manors nationalised, introduced eight-hour working days and organised local soviets. It worked closely with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Lenin</a>'s government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> but had little time to build institutions, and Latvian nationalists rejected it as illegitimate.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In February 1918 the German army advanced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-faustschlag">Operation Faustschlag</a> and occupied all of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Latvia</a>, and Iskolat fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Its leaders later formed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/latvian-socialist-soviet-republic">Latvian Socialist Soviet Republic</a> in December 1918, while nationalists proclaimed the Republic of Latvia.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/iskolat.jpg" alt="Iskolat" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Abols (Jānis Āboliņš); derivative work by Explorer09 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iskolata_karogs.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1917 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Valka</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latvian, Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a>, <a href="/countries/estonia">Estonia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Iskolat Republic, Executive Committee of the Soviets of Latvia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=iskolat" aria-label="Iskolat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5236 224 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Iskolat lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5556 480L5520 501M5556 480L5820 533M5556 480L5473 630"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5520 501h0M5820 533h0M5473 630h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5556 480h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5556 480h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Valka. <a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=iskolat">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
